## Hard-won lessons (2026-05-17 — transparent header session)

### Lesson 1: `position: sticky` always reserves physical space
Even when `background: transparent`, a sticky element holds its height in the document flow. The body background (`#0f172a`) fills that space and creates a visible dark band. **Fix:** switch to `position: fixed` on workspace, set workspace to `height: 100dvh`, add `padding-top` to the message list so content starts below the floating bars.

### Lesson 2: `el.style.right = ""` does NOT override a CSS class rule
Clearing an inline style with `""` removes the inline value, letting the CSS class rule take over again. If a class sets `right: 6px` and you want to override it with `left`, set `el.style.right = "auto"` — not `""`.

### Lesson 3: "Transparent over same-color background" looks solid
If the page background and the area behind the transparent element are the same color (`#0f172a`), removing the fill looks identical. The transparency only becomes visible when content of a different color (e.g. message bubbles) scrolls behind the element. Show the user this by scrolling chat content behind the pills.

### Lesson 4: Use `body.ws-active` CSS class, not JS `style.display`
Toggling `display: none` via JS breaks `offsetHeight` measurements and `--vc-app-shell-height`. Instead, add/remove a body class (`document.body.classList.toggle('ws-active', screenName === 'workspace')`) and use CSS selectors to change layout per screen.

### Lesson 5: The VC DEBUG overlay blocks Playwright clicks
The debug overlay has `pointer-events: auto` and covers the lower screen. Always use `?vc_debug=0` in the Playwright URL to disable it before automating clicks.
